From 14df94cca2b82c7ae37d8f0d5e6f4da7fc6c65d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:26:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/25] l2r-local: entry 23-b: ir: rc.defer and drop.run,
 lowered to the drop stack

Second step of the in-place worklist for RecordDropKind::Worklist drops
(23-a added the runtime stack). Two ops let drop glue use the stack;
nothing emits them yet.

- `reussir.rc.defer @release(%box)`: hands a box whose last reference
  the caller owns (count observed as one, not decremented) to the
  thread's stack; the drain later calls `@release(%box)`, which releases
  the content and frees the box. Outside a drain the release runs at
  once (23-a's `defer`).
- `reussir.drop.run @walk(%ref)`: calls `@walk(%ref)` with the thread
  draining, then (outermost only) runs what it deferred. A nested one (in
  a release or a foreign hook the drain runs) returns before the value's
  dead members are released; the running drain releases them.

Both name their callee by symbol; the verifier requires a function that
takes exactly the operand's type and returns nothing, and a shared box
for rc.defer (rigid and flex boxes are not counted). The basic-ops lowering turns
them into `__reussir_drop_defer(box, @release)` and
`__reussir_drop_run(ref, @walk)` (new DropStackLowering.cpp), declaring
the two runtime functions only in modules that use them, `nounwind`
only, since the callbacks run arbitrary drop glue.

Tests: conversion/drop_stack_ops.mlir (round trip and lowering),
conversion/drop_stack_invalid.mlir (verifier, rigid and flex boxes
included).
